WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
US sugar tariffs meant to protect farmers instead pushed manufacturers toward high-fruit

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.820
toast corn syrup, reshaping American diets.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.180
This is unintended consequences, episode 114.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:17.920
In the decades after World War II, Congress set import quotas on sugar to keep domestic

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.800
cane and beet prices well above world levels.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.960
Food companies facing those higher costs, soon discovered that corn, once processed through

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.380
new enzymatic methods, could yield a sweet and reprised noticeably lower.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:38.220
The policy succeeded in its narrow aim of supporting growers, yet it quietly redirected an entire

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.720
processing industry toward a substitute few had anticipated.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.640
Manufacturers did not abandon sugar out of preference for a new ingredient.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.160
They followed the arithmetic of delivered cost once the enzymatic route became reliable

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.180
at plant scale.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:59.080
The same quota that stabilized one set of farm prices therefore opened a durable price

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.560
gap that corn refiners were positioned to fill.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.620
After the war, lawmakers viewed stable domestic sugar production as essential for food security

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:08.840
and rural economies.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.940
Cain growers in Florida, Louisiana and Hawaii, along with beet farmers across the Midwest, faced

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:21.380
competition from lower cost imports that could swing with weather, currency, and foreign subsidies.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:27.540
Quotas and tariffs offered a straightforward remedy, limits supply from abroad so that American

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.100
producers could sell at prices that covered their costs and sustain their communities.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.380
At the time, this approach aligned with broader agricultural policy that treated price

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.620
supports as a practical way to preserve farmland and processing capacity.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.760
The decision reflected the information available.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.440
World sugar markets had proven volatile, and domestic output was seen as a buffer worth

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.820
maintaining. Lawmakers could point to wartime experience when import disruptions had threatened

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:02.340
candy, canning, and soft drink lines. Shielding growers therefore looked like prudent continuity rather

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:08.540
than an experiment in market design. The quota system operated through import limits that held U.S.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:14.620
sugar prices roughly double or triple world levels for years. Early results appeared positive for

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.440
the targeted growers who expanded acreage and modernized equipment under the protected prices.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.740
Food manufacturers continued using sugar where tradition or labeling required it, while quietly

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:32.220
testing alternatives. By the 1970s advances in enzymatic conversion made it possible to turn

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:38.920
cornstarch into high fructose corn syrup at industrial scale. Companies began adopting the new sweetener

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:44.340
in soft drinks and processed foods once cost calculations favored it over tariff supported sugar.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.500
Proponents of the quota's continued to emphasize farm stability.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.220
Few public voices at the time connected the price gap directly to a coming shift in sweetener

00:02:54.220 --> 00:03:00.280
chemistry. The enzymatic step itself required only modest retrofits inside existing wet

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.900
milling facilities already built for starch and glucose production. So the capital barrier proved

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.200
lower than building new cane refineries would have been. Because the quota's kept sugar expensive,

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.660
any viable substitute gained an immediate price advantage. Once high fructose corn syrup reached

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:23.600
commercial viability, beverage makers and packaged food producers switched rapidly to capture the savings.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:32.380
Per capita HFCS consumption moved from near zero in 1970 to more than 60 pounds by the late 1990s.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:38.020
The change was not limited to one product category. It rippled through the entire sweetener

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:44.520
supply chain encouraging expanded corn acreage, new wet milling plants and reformulated recipes

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:50.980
across thousands of items. Second order effects appeared in agricultural markets as corn demand rose

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:56.560
while sugar beet and cane acreage faced different pressures. Third order effects touched public

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.280
health patterns as overall sweetener intake increased and the composition of that intake changed.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:08.520
The original policy never required or encouraged the corn syrup route. It simply altered relative

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:14.560
prices enough that rational firms followed the lower cost path. No single actor planned the dietary

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.800
shift, yet the incentive structure made it the predictable outcome of protecting one commodity

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:25.900
without accounting for ready substitutes. Even when later studies noted the coincidence with rising

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:31.980
obesity rates, the quota machinery had already locked in a multi-billion dollar corn refining sector.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:38.100
Whose economics now depended on continued price separation from sugar. By the time consumption

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:43.560
data showed the scale of the change, the quota system remained in place with only modest adjustments.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:49.700
Some manufacturers later reduced HFCS use in response to consumer preference and labeling

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:56.300
concerns, yet the underlying price differential continued to shape choices. No comprehensive reversal

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:02.180
of the original tariffs occurred. Instead, policy attention turned to broader farm bills and trade

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:08.980
agreements that sometimes eased sometimes maintained protection levels. Attempts to revisit sugar policy

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:14.880
therefore encountered an established wet milling lobby whose plants once built could not be unbuilt

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.380
without stranding capital that had already followed the price signal created by the quotas.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.740
Incentive structures always find their loopholes, often through the nearest available substitute

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:30.940
rather than outright evasion. Complex systems resist simple interventions because protecting one

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:36.840
input can quietly reorder entire production networks. When designing price or supply rules,

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:41.500
it helps to map the closest technical alternatives before the policy locks in.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:47.680
How might today's efforts to support specific crops or materials similarly steer industries

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:53.260
toward unforeseen replacements? We'll leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking through this one

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:58.920
with me. If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way, and I'll see you

